[{"category":"section","location":"#JessamineBenchmark","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for JessamineBenchmark.\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark"},{"category":"module","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.SinFunction","page":"Home","text":"Generate data from sin\n\nTo run this CLI app manually:\n\njulia --project=@. -m JessamineBenchmark.SinFunction\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.SinFunction"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.SinFunction.make_rand_sin-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":" make_rand_sin(kwargs...)\n\nMake random samples of the input x as columns and apply sin to the result. Make additional random samples as columns to use as distractors.\n\nKeywords\n\nrng=Random.default_rng(): Random number generator to use.\nnum_samples=100: How many points (rows) to create.\ninput_dist=Uniform(-pi, pi): Distribution of inputs.\nnoise_dist=Dirac(0): Distribution of noise; default means no noise.\n\nReturns\n\nA DataFrame with the following columns:\n\ny: result of applying sin to input x plus noise.\nx: input\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.SinFunction.make_rand_sin"},{"category":"module","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions","page":"Home","text":"Generate data from Friedman functions, which are commonly used for machine learning benchmarks.\n\nIn Friedman's original article, friedman1 is (61), friedman2 is (63a), and friedman3 is (63b).\n\nBreiman, Leo. Bagging Predictors. Machine Learning, v24 no2 p123–140, 1996-08-01. doi:10.1007/BF00058655\n\nFriedman, Jerome H. Multivariate adaptive regression splines. The Annals of Statistics v19 no1 p1–67, 1991. JStor\n\nThis module follows the Scikit-learn implementation\n\nTo run this CLI app manually:\n\njulia --project=@. -m JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.friedman1-NTuple{5, Any}","page":"Home","text":"friedman1(x1, x2, x3, x4, x5)\n\nReturn 10 sin(π x_1 x_2) + 20 (x_3 - 12)^2 + 10 x_4 + 5 x_5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.friedman1"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.friedman2-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Home","text":"friedman2(x1, x2, x3, x4)\n\nReturn sqrtx_1^2 + left(x_2 x_3 - frac1x_2 x4right).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.friedman2"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.friedman3-NTuple{4, Any}","page":"Home","text":"friedman3(x1, x2, x3, x4)\n\nReturn atan left(fracx_2 x_3 - 1(x_2 x_4)x_1right).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.friedman3"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.make_rand_friedman1-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":" make_rand_friedman1(kwargs...)\n\nMake random samples of the inputs x_1 dots x_5 as columns and apply friedman1 to the result. Make additional random samples as columns to use as distractors.\n\nKeywords\n\nrng=Random.default_rng(): Random number generator to use.\nnum_samples=100: How many points (rows) to create.\nnum_distractors=0: How many distractor columns to create.\ninput_dist=Uniform(): Distribution of inputs and distractors.\nnoise_dist=Dirac(0): Distribution of noise; default means no noise.\n\nReturns\n\nA DataFrame with the following columns:\n\ny: result of applying friedman1 to inputs x1, ..., x5, plus noise.\nx1, ..., x5: the five inputs\nx6, ... : distractors\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.make_rand_friedman1"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.make_rand_friedman2-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"make_rand_friedman2(kwargs...)\n\nMake random samples of the inputs x_1 dots x_5 as columns and apply friedman2 to the result. Make additional random samples as columns to use as distractors.\n\nKeywords\n\nrng=Random.default_rng(): Random number generator to use.\nnum_samples=100: How many points (rows) to create.\nx1_dist=Uniform(0, 100): Distribution for x1.\nx2_dist=Uniform(2*π*40, 2*π*280): Distribution for x2.\nx3_dist=Uniform(0, 1): Distribution for x3\nx4_dist=Uniform(1, 11): Distribution for x4.\nnoise_dist=Dirac(0): Distribution of noise; default means no noise.\n\nReturns\n\nA DataFrame with the following columns:\n\ny: result of applying friedman2 to inputs x1, ..., x4, plus noise.\nx1, ..., x4: the four inputs\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.make_rand_friedman2"},{"category":"method","location":"#JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.make_rand_friedman3-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"make_rand_friedman3(kwargs...)\n\nMake random samples of the inputs x_1 dots x_5 as columns and apply friedman3 to the result. Make additional random samples as columns to use as distractors.\n\nKeywords\n\nrng=Random.default_rng(): Random number generator to use.\nnum_samples=100: How many points (rows) to create.\nx1_dist=Uniform(0, 100): Distribution for x1.\nx2_dist=Uniform(2*π*40, 2*π*280): Distribution for x2.\nx3_dist=Uniform(0, 1): Distribution for x3\nx4_dist=Uniform(1, 11): Distribution for x4.\nnoise_dist=Dirac(0): Distribution of noise; default means no noise.\n\nReturns\n\nA DataFrame with the following columns:\n\ny: result of applying friedman3 to inputs x1, ..., x4, plus noise.\nx1, ..., x4: the four inputs\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"JessamineBenchmark.FriedmanFunctions.make_rand_friedman3"}]
